vmlab (this lab), 2026-11-13: yttrium: forced-sample coverage as one draw per sample

Word's ribbon had thin pale gaps in its rounded boxes (the search box, the
Styles gallery), in different places at every start of Word. They lie on the
inner edges of the triangle fans Direct2D draws its shapes with.

Direct2D on feature level 11.1 antialiases shapes with target-independent
rasterization: it draws the triangles with ForcedSampleCount 16 into a
single-sampled R16_UINT texture, the pixel shader writes SV_Coverage and the
output merger XORs it in. This driver rasterized such a draw once, at 16
samples and with no attachment, and XORed in the fragment shader under a
pixel interlock (VK_EXT_fragment_shader_interlock). With this lab's host
(NVIDIA RTX 4050, driver 595.99, through Venus) the gaps are what a lock gives
that does not hold between two fragments of one pixel whose coverage is
disjoint, which is every pixel on an edge two triangles share: both read the
old value and one XOR is lost. Ordered and unordered interlock give the same
gaps; with no interlock at all whole blocks are wrong, so the lock does hold
between fragments that share samples. Whether the NVIDIA driver or the Venus
renderer is at fault was not looked into.

Now such a draw goes out as one single-sampled draw per sample, to the
texture as an ordinary colour attachment: the viewport is moved so that the
pixel centre falls on that sample's position (D3D11's standard pattern), and
the blender's logic op XORs that sample's bit, in primitive order and with no
lock. One pipeline serves all the draws: each draw's viewport has its depth
range collapsed to (sample + 0.5) / 16, and the fragment shader takes its
SV_Coverage bit from the fragment depth. This is exact for what Direct2D
does (the shader's output is XORed, so it is linear in the coverage bits).

D3D10UMD_YTTRIUM_FORCED_SAMPLE_PASSES=0 (or FORCED_SAMPLE_PASSES=0 in
C:\ProgramData\Yttrium\yttrium.ini) brings the interlock path back.

Applies after patches/mesa of vmlab-win3d 45ec46ca (release 0.1.2-alpha) and
9001.
Test: start Word (16.0.20527) three times and compare the ribbon: no pixel
differs between the starts, and 5 pixels differ from feature level 11.0
(ENABLE_FL11_1=0), where Direct2D does not use this path. Before: 260 to 370
pixels differed from start to start.

